part 1&2
1. cgroup Driver Mismatch (SystemdCgroup)
This is a real-world production gotcha. Being able to explain why two cgroup managers cause instability, what SystemdCgroup = true does, and citing the Kubernetes docs shows deep understanding. Most people just copy-paste this without knowing why.

2. API Server Advertise Address
Explaining why --apiserver-advertise-address matters in a multi-interface environment (NAT vs private network), how it affects TLS certificate generation, and how the join command is constructed from it — this shows you understand Kubernetes networking fundamentals.

3. Idempotent Provisioning
The if [ ! -f /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf ] check shows you think about infrastructure as code properly — scripts should be safe to run multiple times without breaking things. This is a DevOps mindset question interviewers love.

4. Debugging etcd and API Server Crashes
Walking through crictl ps -a, reading container logs, identifying etcd being killed, tracing it to resource starvation and cgroup misconfiguration — this shows a systematic debugging approach.

5. Windows CRLF Line Endings
Simple but common in real teams. Knowing why it breaks Linux scripts and how to fix it with sed shows cross-platform awareness.

The cgroup driver and API server advertise address topics are the strongest — they show you understand Kubernetes internals, not just how to follow a tutorial.

6. Calico CNI Installation Timing
Understanding that the API server needs to be fully ready before applying CNI manifests, and replacing a brittle sleep with a proper readiness loop shows you understand Kubernetes bootstrap order and how to write reliable automation.

7. kubeconfig Management
Understanding why admin.conf exists, who needs access to it, setting it up for multiple users (vagrant, root, current user), and why -i vs -f matters in automated scripts. Also understanding KUBECONFIG environment variable and when to use it explicitly.

8. Container Runtime Troubleshooting
Using crictl ps -a to find crashed containers, reading container logs with crictl logs, checking static pod manifests in /etc/kubernetes/manifests/ — these are real skills that show you can debug at the container runtime level, not just with kubectl.

9. Vagrant Multi-Machine Networking
Understanding how Vagrant's NAT and host-only adapters work, why the default interface causes problems for Kubernetes, and how private network IPs need to be consistent across the cluster.

10. Infrastructure Sizing
Starting with 1028MB RAM and learning through failure that Kubernetes control plane needs at minimum 2GB, and why CPU starvation causes kernel-level issues like rcu_sched timeouts — this shows operational awareness.

part 3 (spent 1-2 hour debugging)
1.) Removed the unnecessary sleep 240 and redundant kubeconfig flags, replacing them with a proper readiness check.
2.) Centralized and fixed kubeconfig setup via /vagrant/set-kubeconfig.sh with a consistent KUBECONFIG export.
3.) Standardized kubectl usage and ordering so commands run only after the API server is reachable and properly configured.
